=== WHY THE 4 RETRY PAIRS DID NOT STOP THE LEG (the contrast with 10-09 is the point) ===
On 2026-10-09 the leg DIED on status `error`, because the driver classes `error` transport-class
(r47_sa_run2.out lines 117 and 121) and spent both of its `--auto-resume-transient` retries on it before
`STOP sa: error`. `contract_unavailable` is handled differently by the same driver: each pair is kept for
retry at the PER-PAIR 3-attempt budget ("kept for retry (contract_unavailable, attempt 1 of 3)") and NO
`RESUME` line fired, so the leg-level auto-resume budget was never drawn down. The checkpoint's
`auto_resumes` list is unchanged since 2026-10-09. That is why 4 unavailable pairs end as a completion
with `stopped` null instead of a second stop at 238.

=== WHAT THIS RECORD DOES NOT CLAIM ===
- NOT that all 242 pairs were collected. 237 were. The other 5 are listed above with their own statuses.
- NOT that the 4 `contract_unavailable` pairs are permanently unavailable. Each is at attempt 1 of 3, so
  a later run may collect them. Whether to re-run for them is the issuer's call, not this seat's.
- NOT a judgment on AC3. That is `3.0.0_SA_CVO`'s at L9, from this record.
- The 0xC0000142 collector-launch fault that caused the 10-09 stop did NOT recur: 0 occurrences of
  rc=3221225794 and 0 `run_id=None` across the whole 32-hour run, against 101 uploads with 0 failed
  (on 10-09 all 4 uploads failed). Row AMGT_HARVST-Z-S113's finding about the mislabelling stands; only
  its recurrence question is answered.
